{{Politics of Okaiken}}

The '''Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry''' ({{lang-ja|経済産業省}}, {{Nihongo|Keizai Sangyō-shō|けいざいさんぎょうしょう}}, universally abbreviated '''METI''') is a cabinet-level ministry of the [[Government of Okaiken]] responsible for the formulation and execution of national industrial policy, international trade negotiations, domestic manufacturing strategy, commercial energy regulation, and technological standards. The ministry oversees an operational budget of approximately 48 billion Sen and directs a staff of 32,400 economic civil servants across its headquarters in Kasumigaseki and its regional bureaus.

The ministry oversees state policy toward the major corporate conglomerates (keiretsu), as well as strategic manufacturing sectors spanning automotive fabrication, commercial shipbuilding, and advanced semiconductor foundries. The incumbent Minister of Economy, Trade and Industry is [[Tetsuya Maeda]] of the [[Sanese Communist Party]] (SCP), who took office on 20 May 2026 within the [[Ikazaki Cabinet]]. In sharp contrast to previous administrations that prioritized monopolistic corporate subsidies to contractors such as [[Kizan Systems]], Minister Maeda has redirected ministry policy toward industrial democracy, aggressive antitrust enforcement, and direct technical capitalization for small and medium-sized enterprise (SME) supply-chain networks.

==Historical development and industrial transformation==

===The MITI era and the post-war miracle===

The institutional predecessor of METI was the legendary Ministry of International Trade and Industry (通商産業省, ''Tsūshō Sangyō-shō''), commonly known as MITI, founded in 1949 during post-war economic reconstruction. MITI was the principal administrative architect of the Sanese Industrial Miracle, deploying administrative guidance (行政指導, ''gyōsei shidō'') to direct state credit from the central bank into strategic heavy industries, shielding domestic manufacturing behind import tariffs, and orchestrating export cartels in steel, precision machinery, and automobiles.

During the late twentieth century, MITI's close triangular relationship with ruling politicians and keiretsu executives—often described by economic historians as the Iron Triangle—fostered immense industrial capacity but also generated severe systemic vulnerabilities. The speculative asset bubble collapse in 1991 and the Corporate Winter bribery scandals of the mid-1990s exposed deep regulatory capture and cronyism, accelerating parliamentary demands for structural reform.

===The 2001 restructuring and the industrial sovereignty doctrine===

On 6 January 2001, the National Diet enacted the 2001 Central Government Reform, restructuring MITI into METI. The reform formally integrated energy policy into the department through the elevation of the Agency for Natural Resources and Energy and established the [[Small and Medium Enterprise Agency (Okaiken)|Small and Medium Enterprise Agency]] as an external bureau. Throughout the 2000s and 2010s, METI articulated the Industrial Sovereignty (産業主権, ''Sangyō Shuken'') doctrine, advocating that the state maintain sovereign domestic control over strategic supply chains, critical computing chips, and primary energy networks.

However, this doctrine was distorted during the conservative Honda administration, which poured hundreds of billions of Sen in state subsidies into Kizan Systems to develop proprietary semiconductor architectures and the [[Satori AI]] software platform. When Kizan's algorithmic network suffered catastrophic failure during the May 2026 Satori AI Blackout, national industrial manufacturing ground to a halt. In the aftermath, the Ikazaki Cabinet launched a criminal corporate investigation into Kizan procurement fraud and enacted the [[Digital Sovereignty Act (Okaiken)]], mandating that METI break up proprietary hardware monopolies and establish open-architecture industrial standards.

