==Antitrust enforcement and supply-chain resilience==

Under Minister Tetsuya Maeda, METI's primary strategic focus in late 2026 has been dismantling the monopolistic procurement networks that previously favored politically connected keiretsu conglomerates. Working in statutory coordination with the Fair Trade Commission, the ministry issued the 2026 Industrial Decentralization Directive, requiring large automotive and electronics manufacturers to eliminate exclusive supplier contracts that barred subcontractors from selling precision components to third parties.

Concurrently, the ministry directs the National Urban Mining Programme through the [[Okaiken Urban Mining Corporation]]. Because Okaiken relies heavily on imported rare earth elements for electric vehicle motors and electronic circuitry, METI established municipal recycling mandates that extract high-purity lithium, cobalt, and neodymium from discarded consumer electronics, securing domestic industrial mineral reserves independent of foreign supply blockades.

==Intergovernmental relations with prefectural and metropolitan governments==

While industrial and foreign trade policies are established at the national level, the execution of economic development in Okaiken depends on close administrative partnerships between METI, the forty-seven provincial prefectures, and [[Okami Metropolis]]. Because regional economies vary widely—ranging from heavy metallurgical bases in the west to agricultural processing in the northeast—the ministry operates through an extensive decentralized field presence.

METI projects its authority into the provinces through eight Regional Bureaus of Economy, Trade and Industry (経済産業局). These regional bureaus coordinate directly with Prefectural Departments of Commerce, Industry and Labour (商工労働部). Regional bureaus assist prefectural governors in designating Special Industrial Revitalization Zones, packaging central infrastructure subsidies and corporate tax exemptions to attract precision manufacturing plants into depopulating prefectures such as [[Miyagi Prefecture]] and [[Tokuyama Prefecture]].

The primary conduit supporting local economies is the [[Small and Medium Enterprise Agency (Okaiken)|Small and Medium Enterprise Agency]] (SMEA). SMEA works in direct statutory partnership with Prefectural Credit Guarantee Corporations (信用保証協会) and local Chambers of Commerce and Industry. When regional small manufacturers face credit contraction or raw material inflation, the central agency underwrites municipal credit guarantees, shielding regional family-owned machine shops and parts suppliers from bankruptcy.

In energy grid regulation, METI's Agency for Natural Resources and Energy must balance national electrical transmission baselines against prefectural environmental and safety demands. Prefectural governors hold significant political influence over the siting and restarting of regional thermal power plants, commercial wind farms, and hydroelectric dams. Minister Maeda established the National Energy Federal Council, conferring monthly with prefectural governors to negotiate regional renewable energy feed-in tariffs and coordinate electrical grid load balancing during summer peak demands.

Relations with the [[Okami Metropolitan Government]] occupy a distinct tier of economic administration. Okami Metropolis represents the financial, corporate, and retail capital of the nation, housing the global headquarters of the keiretsu and over forty percent of all registered corporate capital. METI coordinates with the Okami Metropolitan Bureau of Industrial and Labor Affairs on urban zoning for technological incubators, foreign venture capital attraction, and the regulation of consumer credit markets across the metropolitan core.

